# The ledger of `ipc`: what a program can do to talk with another process of
# the same machine, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. The
# lists are this machine's, 2026-11-17: Go 1.27's `go doc -all net` (its
# `Unix*` entries) and `golang.org/x/sys` v0.46.0 read by `go doc -all` for
# unix (darwin and linux) and windows; Rust 1.100's `std::os::unix::net` and
# `std::os::linux::net` documentation (the methods of each struct, read from
# the documentation's own anchors); Python 3.15's `dir()` of
# `multiprocessing.connection` (and its `Connection` and `Listener`),
# `multiprocessing.shared_memory` (and `SharedMemory`, `ShareableList`),
# `mmap` (and `mmap.mmap`) and `socket`'s local-socket names. `posix_ipc` is
# not installed here; its lines name the capabilities its documentation
# gives, as the author's request named it, and so do the `rust` lines that
# name the `interprocess` crate, the request's yardstick (no crate source is
# on this machine, and none was opened).

capability ipc/listen-path  a listener on a socket file at a path
  heroes  ipc.listen
  go      net.ListenUnix, net.Listen (network "unix"), net.ResolveUnixAddr, net.UnixAddr, net.UnixAddr.Network, net.UnixAddr.String, net.UnixListener
  rust    UnixListener::bind, UnixListener::bind_addr, SocketAddr::from_pathname, interprocess::local_socket::ListenerOptions, interprocess::local_socket::GenericFilePath
  python  socket.AF_UNIX, socket.socket.bind, socket.socket.listen, multiprocessing.connection.Listener, multiprocessing.connection.SocketListener, multiprocessing.connection.families, multiprocessing.connection.default_family, multiprocessing.connection.address_type
  note    the same door on Darwin, Linux and Windows 10 1803 and later (AF_UNIX there too; the Windows arm compiled against MinGW-w64's `afunix.h` and unrun on the box on 2026-11-17)

capability ipc/listen-permission  who may connect, by the socket file's permission
  heroes  ipc.listen
  go      - (os.Chmod after net.Listen)
  rust    - (std::fs::set_permissions after bind)
  python  - (os.chmod after bind)
  note    the permission is a parameter, set between the bind and the listen, so no client connects while the file has the mask's bits (Go, Rust and Python leave that gap to the program); 0o600 lets only this user connect. Windows has no such bits and ignores it

capability ipc/stale-socket-file  a socket file a process that died left at a listener's path
  heroes  ipc.listen, ipc.close_listener
  go      net.UnixListener.SetUnlinkOnClose, net.UnixListener.Close
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    as Go does (observed 2026-11-17: `listen` on a stale file fails "address already in use" and leaves it; `Close` removes the file it made): `listen` fails `exists` and leaves the file, and `close_listener` removes the file where it is still the one `listen` made (its device and inode compared). A connect to such a file fails `not_found` with words saying nothing listens there (ECONNREFUSED, measured on Darwin and Linux). Go's unlink-on-close cannot be turned off here: a listener that leaves its file behind is the stale file itself

capability ipc/accept  the next connection on a listener, with a bound on the wait
  heroes  ipc.accept
  go      net.UnixListener.Accept, net.UnixListener.AcceptUnix, net.UnixListener.SetDeadline
  rust    UnixListener::accept, UnixListener::incoming, Incoming, interprocess::local_socket::Listener::accept
  python  multiprocessing.connection.Listener.accept, multiprocessing.connection.Listener.last_accepted, socket.socket.accept
  note    the bound is given at the call, `within_ms`, where Go sets a deadline on the listener: one call, one bound, and a close from another thread ends the wait within 50 ms. The peer's address of an accepted local socket is unnamed on every platform, so `accept` answers the stream alone, and `peer_of` says who connected

capability ipc/connect  a connection to a listener at a path
  heroes  ipc.connect
  go      net.DialUnix, net.Dial (network "unix"), net.Dialer.DialUnix
  rust    UnixStream::connect, UnixStream::connect_addr, interprocess::local_socket::Stream::connect
  python  multiprocessing.connection.Client, multiprocessing.connection.SocketClient, socket.socket.connect, multiprocessing.connection.CONNECTION_TIMEOUT
  note    nothing at the path and a socket file nobody listens on are both `not_found`, their messages apart; `within_ms` bounds the wait while the listener's queue is full (Linux answers EAGAIN then, asked again in steps)

capability ipc/channel-bytes  a channel's bytes read and written
  heroes  io.read, io.write
  go      net.UnixConn.Read, net.UnixConn.Write
  rust    UnixStream (Read, Write), interprocess::local_socket::Stream (Read, Write)
  python  socket.socket.recv, socket.socket.send, socket.socket.sendall
  note    a channel is an `io.Stream` (`hero_stream_make` over the member's table), so `io` reads, writes, buffers and copies it; nothing here reads a channel. A write to a peer gone fails `closed`, never a signal

capability ipc/channel-peek  bytes looked at without taking them
  heroes  io.peek
  go      -
  rust    UnixStream::peek
  python  -
  note    a reader of `io` buffers and peeks; no second way here

capability ipc/channel-close  a channel closed
  heroes  io.close
  go      net.UnixConn.Close
  rust    - (drop)
  python  multiprocessing.connection.Connection.close, multiprocessing.connection.Connection.closed, socket.socket.close

capability ipc/listener-close  a listener closed
  heroes  ipc.close_listener
  go      net.UnixListener.Close
  rust    - (drop)
  python  multiprocessing.connection.Listener.close

capability ipc/channel-deadline  a channel's reads and writes bounded in time
  heroes  ipc.set_deadline
  go      net.UnixConn.SetDeadline, net.UnixConn.SetReadDeadline, net.UnixConn.SetWriteDeadline
  rust    UnixStream::set_read_timeout, UnixStream::set_write_timeout, UnixStream::read_timeout, UnixStream::write_timeout
  python  socket.socket.settimeout
  note    a time, as Go's and `net.set_deadline`, never a span per call: a peer that sends a byte before each limit still meets it. One deadline for both directions, as `net` keeps it; Go's two halves were not asked for by any other Rebel package

capability ipc/channel-half-close  a channel's writing side closed while it still reads
  heroes  ipc.close_write
  go      net.UnixConn.CloseWrite, net.UnixConn.CloseRead
  rust    UnixStream::shutdown
  python  socket.socket.shutdown
  note    the writing side alone: the reading side ends with `io.close`. A Windows named pipe has no half and fails `unsupported`

capability ipc/peer-credentials  who is at the other end of a channel
  heroes  ipc.peer_of
  go      golang.org/x/sys/unix.GetsockoptUcred, golang.org/x/sys/unix.GetsockoptXucred, golang.org/x/sys/windows.GetNamedPipeClientProcessId, golang.org/x/sys/windows.GetNamedPipeServerProcessId
  rust    UnixStream::peer_cred, UCred
  python  socket.SO_PEERCRED, socket.LOCAL_PEERCRED, socket.socket.getsockopt
  note    the process id and the user id, read once when the connection is made and kept: Darwin answers no peer once it has closed its side (ENOTCONN, measured 2026-11-17), where Linux keeps the credentials of the connect. Linux by SO_PEERCRED, Darwin by LOCAL_PEERPID and getpeereid, Windows by SIO_AF_UNIX_GETPEERPID and the pipe's process ids, its user -1 (unrun on the box)

capability ipc/addresses  a socket's own address and its peer's
  heroes  ipc.Listener
  go      net.UnixConn.LocalAddr, net.UnixConn.RemoteAddr, net.UnixListener.Addr
  rust    UnixListener::local_addr, UnixStream::local_addr, UnixStream::peer_addr, SocketAddr::as_pathname, SocketAddr::is_unnamed
  python  socket.socket.getsockname, socket.socket.getpeername, multiprocessing.connection.Listener.address
  note    a listener keeps the path or name it was made on in its `name` and `space`; a connection's own and peer's addresses are unnamed for the side that connected and the side that accepted, so who connected is `peer_of`'s

capability ipc/abstract-names  a channel named in Linux's abstract space, which no file holds
  heroes  ipc.listen_abstract, ipc.connect_abstract
  go      net.Listen and net.Dial with a name beginning "@" (Linux; `go doc net`, UnixAddr)
  rust    SocketAddr::from_abstract_name, SocketAddr::as_abstract_name, std::os::linux::net::SocketAddrExt, interprocess::local_socket::GenericNamespaced
  python  socket.AF_UNIX with an address beginning "\0"
  note    Linux alone (measured in the Linux arm64 container: listen, connect); `unsupported` elsewhere, Windows' abstract names left unasked. The name follows `is_name`'s rule

capability ipc/named-pipes  a channel on a Windows named pipe, `\\.\pipe\<name>`
  heroes  ipc.listen_pipe, ipc.connect_pipe
  go      golang.org/x/sys/windows.CreateNamedPipe, golang.org/x/sys/windows.ConnectNamedPipe, golang.org/x/sys/windows.DisconnectNamedPipe, golang.org/x/sys/windows.GetNamedPipeInfo, golang.org/x/sys/windows.GetNamedPipeHandleState, golang.org/x/sys/windows.SetNamedPipeHandleState
  rust    interprocess::os::windows::named_pipe (PipeListenerOptions, DuplexPipeStream)
  python  multiprocessing.connection.Listener and Client with an address "\\.\pipe\..." (family "AF_PIPE"), multiprocessing.connection.arbitrary_address
  note    a second Windows door beside AF_UNIX, said so: byte mode, overlapped, local clients alone, the first instance claiming the name (a second listener fails `exists`), a client that may be identified and never impersonated (`SECURITY_IDENTIFICATION`). One accept at a time on a listener. `unsupported` off Windows. Its arm is compiled and unrun (2026-11-17)

capability ipc/named-pipe-messages  a Windows pipe's message mode, each write read as one message
  heroes  refused: a message is framed by `write_message` on every platform and every kind of stream; a mode of one platform's pipes would be a second way
  go      -
  rust    interprocess::os::windows::named_pipe (message streams)
  python  - (multiprocessing.connection frames its own)

capability ipc/messages  a message written whole and read back whole
  heroes  ipc.write_message, ipc.read_message
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.connection.Connection.send_bytes, multiprocessing.connection.Connection.recv_bytes, multiprocessing.connection.Connection.recv_bytes_into, multiprocessing.connection.BufferTooShort, multiprocessing.connection.MESSAGE_LENGTH, multiprocessing.connection.BUFSIZE
  note    Python's framing, observed 2026-11-17 (`abc` is `00000003616263`, the empty message `00000000`, the long form `ffffffff` then 8 bytes read back by `recv_bytes`), so a Heroes and a Python program read each other's. Two decisions where Python differs: a length of -2 or below is refused `invalid`, where Python's `recv_bytes` answered `b''`; and one past `most` is refused before its bytes are read, as Python's `maxlength` refuses ("bad message length"), the stream then left for the program to close. `recv_bytes_into`'s buffer of the caller's has no Heroes form: a read answers a list

capability ipc/objects  a value of the language sent whole, pickled
  heroes  refused: no pickle and no reflection; a message's bytes are made by `json`, `msgpack`, `cbor` or `protobuf`, then `write_message`
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.connection.Connection.send, multiprocessing.connection.Connection.recv, multiprocessing.connection.XmlListener, multiprocessing.connection.XmlClient, multiprocessing.connection.rebuild_connection, multiprocessing.connection.reduce_connection

capability ipc/authentication  a listener and a client proving a shared key to each other
  heroes  refused: who may connect is the socket file's permission, and who did connect is `peer_of`; a challenge over a shared key is a protocol a program writes over a channel with `crypto.hmac`
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.connection.answer_challenge, multiprocessing.connection.deliver_challenge, multiprocessing.connection.AuthenticationError, multiprocessing.connection.Listener (authkey), multiprocessing.connection.Client (authkey)

capability ipc/readiness  a wait on channels for the first with bytes to read
  heroes  pending: `poll` waits on a stream by its descriptor, and a channel is a stream made over a table of this member's, which has none (`hero_stream_descriptor` answers `UNSUPPORTED`); a door from `hero_ipc.h` that `hero_poll.h` can wait on is owed, and `poll` is another package's
  go      - (goroutines over the runtime's poller)
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.connection.Connection.poll, multiprocessing.connection.wait, multiprocessing.connection.Connection.readable, multiprocessing.connection.Connection.writable

capability ipc/pair  two connected ends made within one process
  heroes  net.pair
  go      golang.org/x/sys/unix.Socketpair
  rust    UnixStream::pair, UnixDatagram::pair
  python  socket.socketpair, multiprocessing.connection.Pipe
  note    within one process `net.pair` answers two connected streams on every platform; a pair whose one end goes to a child is passing a stream, the pending block `ipc/pass-stream`

capability ipc/pass-stream  a stream handed to another process over a channel
  heroes  pending: SCM_RIGHTS on POSIX needs a byte of data beside the descriptor, which an `io.Reader` buffering the channel may already have taken, and a stream to hand that has a descriptor (`hero_stream_hold`), where a socket's and a channel's stream have none; Windows' `DuplicateHandle` needs a handle on the receiving process, asked by its id, and a word telling the receiver the number. Neither half was made safe and whole on 2026-11-17, and none is written badly
  go      golang.org/x/sys/unix.UnixRights, golang.org/x/sys/unix.ParseUnixRights, golang.org/x/sys/unix.ParseSocketControlMessage, golang.org/x/sys/unix.Sendmsg, golang.org/x/sys/unix.SendmsgN, golang.org/x/sys/unix.Recvmsg, golang.org/x/sys/unix.SendmsgBuffers, golang.org/x/sys/unix.RecvmsgBuffers, golang.org/x/sys/unix.PidfdGetfd, golang.org/x/sys/windows.DuplicateHandle, net.UnixConn.ReadMsgUnix, net.UnixConn.WriteMsgUnix, net.UnixConn.File, net.UnixListener.File
  rust    SocketAncillary, AncillaryData, AncillaryError, ScmRights, ScmCredentials, Messages, SocketCred, UnixStream::send_vectored_with_ancillary, UnixStream::recv_vectored_with_ancillary, UnixStream::passcred, UnixStream::set_passcred, UnixDatagram::send_vectored_with_ancillary, UnixDatagram::send_vectored_with_ancillary_to, UnixDatagram::recv_vectored_with_ancillary, UnixDatagram::recv_vectored_with_ancillary_from, UnixDatagram::passcred, UnixDatagram::set_passcred
  python  socket.send_fds, socket.recv_fds, socket.SCM_RIGHTS, socket.SCM_CREDS, socket.CMSG_LEN, socket.CMSG_SPACE

capability ipc/datagrams  local datagram and sequenced-packet sockets
  heroes  pending: POSIX has them and Windows' AF_UNIX takes stream sockets alone, so they are a door of POSIX with `unsupported` on Windows, sending to and receiving from an address each call; not written on 2026-11-17
  go      net.ListenUnixgram, net.UnixConn.ReadFrom, net.UnixConn.ReadFromUnix, net.UnixConn.WriteTo, net.UnixConn.WriteToUnix, net.Listen (network "unixpacket")
  rust    UnixDatagram, UnixDatagram::bind, UnixDatagram::bind_addr, UnixDatagram::unbound, UnixDatagram::connect, UnixDatagram::connect_addr, UnixDatagram::send, UnixDatagram::send_to, UnixDatagram::send_to_addr, UnixDatagram::recv, UnixDatagram::recv_from, UnixDatagram::peek, UnixDatagram::peek_from, UnixDatagram::peer_addr, UnixDatagram::local_addr, UnixDatagram::read_timeout, UnixDatagram::write_timeout, UnixDatagram::set_read_timeout, UnixDatagram::set_write_timeout, UnixDatagram::shutdown, UnixDatagram::take_error, UnixDatagram::set_nonblocking, UnixDatagram::try_clone
  python  socket.SOCK_DGRAM and socket.SOCK_SEQPACKET with socket.AF_UNIX

capability ipc/fifo  a named pipe of the file system, `mkfifo`
  heroes  refused: `fs`'s block `fs/special-files` refuses making one (Windows has none, and its pipes are `listen_pipe`'s); one made by another program is a path `fs.open` opens as a stream
  go      golang.org/x/sys/unix.Mkfifo
  rust    interprocess::os::unix::fifo_file
  python  - (os.mkfifo, `fs`'s ledger)

capability ipc/memory-create  memory shared by name, made
  heroes  ipc.create_memory
  go      golang.org/x/sys/windows.CreateFileMapping, golang.org/x/sys/windows.MapViewOfFile, golang.org/x/sys/unix.Mmap (over a descriptor the program opened)
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.shared_memory.SharedMemory (create=True), posix_ipc.SharedMemory (O_CREAT, O_EXCL)
  note    POSIX's `shm_open` name `/<name>`, so Python's `SharedMemory(name=...)` opens what this makes and the other way round; the size is the system's, as Python's `size` answers (observed 2026-11-17: 100 asked is 16384 on macOS, for the maker and an opener alike); a size of 0 is refused, as Python refuses it. On Linux the pages are reserved at the making (`posix_fallocate`), where Python leaves a write to a full `/dev/shm` to end the program with SIGBUS: measured in the container, 200 MB on a `/dev/shm` of 64 MB fails `limit` at once

capability ipc/memory-open  memory shared by name, opened, and its size
  heroes  ipc.open_memory, ipc.Memory
  go      golang.org/x/sys/windows.OpenFileMapping
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.shared_memory.SharedMemory (name), multiprocessing.shared_memory.SharedMemory.name, multiprocessing.shared_memory.SharedMemory.size, posix_ipc.SharedMemory (name)
  note    memory its maker has not sized yet is `not_found`, a client that waits for a server opening again

capability ipc/memory-bytes  shared memory's bytes read and written
  heroes  ipc.read_at, ipc.write_at
  go      - (the []byte golang.org/x/sys/unix.Mmap answers)
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.shared_memory.SharedMemory.buf
  note    by copies, every offset and count held against the size before a byte moves, since Heroes values are copies and no address reaches a program; Python hands a `memoryview` and Go a slice over the mapping

capability ipc/memory-atomics  a word of shared memory read, written, added to and swapped whole, with no lock
  heroes  ipc.load_at, ipc.store_at, ipc.add_at, ipc.swap_at
  go      sync/atomic.LoadInt64, sync/atomic.StoreInt64, sync/atomic.AddInt64, sync/atomic.CompareAndSwapInt64 (on a pointer into the mapping)
  rust    std::sync::atomic::AtomicI64::from_ptr (on a pointer into the mapping)
  python  -
  note    an `i64` at a multiple of 8, sequentially consistent; the sum wraps at 64 bits, as Go's and Rust's do, the one place in Rebel a number wraps, since no process can stop another's. Measured: a thousand adds from each of two processes keep all 2000

capability ipc/memory-close  shared memory unmapped from this process, its name taken away
  heroes  ipc.close_memory, ipc.remove_memory
  go      golang.org/x/sys/windows.UnmapViewOfFile, golang.org/x/sys/unix.Munmap
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.shared_memory.SharedMemory.close, multiprocessing.shared_memory.SharedMemory.unlink, posix_ipc.unlink_shared_memory, posix_ipc.SharedMemory.unlink
  note    on Windows the system takes a name away with its last handle, so `remove_memory` there asks that the name is open and changes nothing, `not_found` where it is not, as POSIX answers. Nothing removes a name at this process's exit: the memory is the program's to remove

capability ipc/memory-list  a list of values laid out in shared memory
  heroes  refused: a layout of values is a format a program writes with `write_at`, `msgpack` or `cbor` among them; a list whose items change size in place is Python's structure
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  multiprocessing.shared_memory.ShareableList, multiprocessing.shared_memory.ShareableList.count, multiprocessing.shared_memory.ShareableList.format, multiprocessing.shared_memory.ShareableList.index

