  rust    -
  python  -

capability ipc/semaphore  a semaphore shared by name: made, opened, taken with a bound, given back, closed, its name taken away
  heroes  ipc.create_semaphore, ipc.open_semaphore, ipc.acquire_semaphore, ipc.release_semaphore, ipc.close_semaphore, ipc.remove_semaphore
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  posix_ipc.Semaphore, posix_ipc.Semaphore.acquire, posix_ipc.Semaphore.release, posix_ipc.Semaphore.close, posix_ipc.Semaphore.unlink, posix_ipc.Semaphore.name, posix_ipc.unlink_semaphore, posix_ipc.O_CREAT, posix_ipc.O_EXCL, posix_ipc.SEMAPHORE_VALUE_MAX, posix_ipc.SEMAPHORE_TIMEOUT_SUPPORTED
  note    `sem_open`'s `/<name>`, a count of 0 to 32,767 at its making (macOS's most; 32,768 refused there, measured). macOS has no `sem_timedwait`: the robust route taken tries again in steps from 0.1 ms to 2 ms, measured over four runs to see a release 1.0 to 1.8 ms late on average and 3.0 ms at worst, at under 1% of a core while it waits; a thread kept in `sem_wait` and given up at the bound was the other route, and it would take a count from the semaphore after its caller had gone. Linux waits in `sem_clockwait` (glibc 2.30), 0.1 to 0.3 ms late; Windows on the object (unrun)

capability ipc/semaphore-value  a semaphore's count read
  heroes  refused: macOS answers no count (`sem_getvalue` fails ENOSYS, measured 2026-11-17), and a count read is stale before it is used
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  posix_ipc.Semaphore.value

capability ipc/lock  a lock between processes by name, taken with a bound and released
  heroes  ipc.open_lock, ipc.acquire_lock, ipc.release_lock, ipc.close_lock, ipc.remove_lock
  go      golang.org/x/sys/unix.Flock, golang.org/x/sys/unix.FcntlFlock, golang.org/x/sys/windows.CreateMutex, golang.org/x/sys/windows.CreateMutexEx, golang.org/x/sys/windows.OpenMutex, golang.org/x/sys/windows.ReleaseMutex, golang.org/x/sys/windows.WaitForSingleObject, golang.org/x/sys/windows.LockFileEx, golang.org/x/sys/windows.UnlockFileEx
  rust    -
  python  - (fcntl.flock; posix_ipc.Semaphore with a count of 1)
  note    on POSIX a lock is the file `/tmp/heroes-lock-<name>` locked whole by `flock`, which the system releases when the holding process ends however it ends; the other route the brief named, a semaphore of one, stays taken forever when its holder is killed. On Windows a mutex. Held by one thread: a release by another is refused `invalid`, and a thread asking a lock it holds is refused at once, where `flock` would let a second call through and a Windows mutex count a second hold. `flock` takes no bound, so the wait steps as macOS's semaphore does (a release seen 1.5 to 2.1 ms late on average and 3.5 ms at worst, measured on Darwin and in the Linux arm64 container)

capability ipc/lock-abandoned  a lock whose holder died, told to its next holder
  heroes  ipc.acquire_lock, ipc.Acquired
  go      golang.org/x/sys/windows.WAIT_ABANDONED
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    on Windows `WAIT_ABANDONED`, a thread's end included; on POSIX the lock's first byte, `H` while held and zero once released, which the next holder reads: measured on Darwin and Linux, a holder killed by SIGKILL left the lock taken `abandoned` within 0.1 ms. What POSIX cannot tell: a holder killed after it took the lock and before it wrote the byte, an instant, reads as clean; and a thread that ends holding a lock while its process runs on leaves it held until the process closes the lock or ends, the hold being the open file's

capability ipc/message-queues  queues of messages by name, kept by the system
  heroes  refused: macOS has no POSIX message queue (`mq_open`) and Windows none; a channel with `write_message` carries messages on every platform
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  posix_ipc.MessageQueue, posix_ipc.unlink_message_queue, posix_ipc.MESSAGE_QUEUES_SUPPORTED

